# Monopoly — Systems Design Document

Author: monopoly-architect (design only; no game code written here)
Target: a playable Monopoly game in Python, runnable as `python main.py`.

---

## 1. Goal & Constraints

Deliver a **playable** Monopoly game as a Python program a person can run and
watch. The runtime is fixed and non-negotiable:

- Entry point is `main.py`, invoked as `python main.py` with **no arguments**
  and **no stdin** (it will not be run interactively).
- Therefore the game must be **self-driving**: it cannot block waiting for
  human input. We satisfy "playable" by shipping a deterministic, seeded
  **auto-play demo** driven by simple AI agents, plus a fully-formed engine
  that a human could drive interactively if a UI were attached later.

The design keeps the engine (rules) strictly separate from the driver (who
decides moves) and the renderer (how state is shown). That separation is what
makes the non-interactive constraint a non-issue: the same engine that an AI
drives could be driven by a human REPL or GUI.

---

## 2. Rules Subset Chosen (and why)

Monopoly has a large rule surface. We implement a **faithful core** that is
unambiguously playable and winnable, and we explicitly document what is
simplified. The guiding principle: implement everything that affects who wins,
and simplify only bookkeeping that does not change outcomes.

### Implemented

- **Board**: the standard 40-space board in canonical order, with the real
  names and prices for the US edition.
- **Properties**: 22 colored properties across 8 color groups, 4 railroads,
  2 utilities.
- **Rent**:
  - Color properties: base rent, rent with 1–4 houses, rent with hotel,
    and **doubled base rent when the owner holds the full color group**
    (monopoly) with no houses.
  - Railroads: 25 / 50 / 100 / 200 by count owned.
  - Utilities: 4× or 10× dice roll by count owned (1 or 2).
- **Buying**: landing on an unowned property lets the player buy at list
  price or decline (declined property stays unowned; no auction — see
  simplifications).
- **Building**: houses/hotels may be built on a full color group, respecting
  **even-build** (no property may exceed another in the group by more than
  one house) and the **house/hotel supply limits** (32 houses, 12 hotels).
  Selling houses back to the bank at half price is supported.
- **Mortgaging**: mortgage for half the price, unmortgage for mortgage value
  + 10% interest. Mortgaged properties collect no rent.
- **Jail**: three ways in (Go To Jail space, "Go to Jail" card, three
  consecutive doubles). Three ways out (pay $50, use a Get Out of Jail Free
  card, roll doubles). After three failed attempts, must pay $50 and move.
- **Chance & Community Chest**: full decks with the standard card effects
  (money, move-to, move-relative, go-to-jail, get-out-of-jail-free, pay-per-
  house, collect-from-each-player, nearest-utility/railroad with special
  rent multipliers).
- **Doubles**: roll again on doubles; three doubles in a row sends you to
  jail.
- **Bankruptcy**: a player who cannot pay is bankrupt; their assets transfer
  to the creditor (or to the bank if the creditor is the bank). Last player
  standing wins.
- **Win condition**: last non-bankrupt player, or a turn cap with the
  richest player (net worth) declared winner (needed for the demo to
  terminate — see §7).

### Explicitly simplified (documented, not hidden)

- **No auctions.** A declined property simply remains unowned. Auctions add
  an interactive bidding loop that is awkward to auto-play and does not
  change the core loop's playability. (Noted as a future extension.)
- **No trading between players.** Trading requires negotiation; the AI does
  not negotiate. This makes full color groups harder to complete, which is
  why the turn cap exists.
- **No Free Parking jackpot.** Free Parking is a no-op (standard modern
  rules).
- **No "even build" enforcement across mortgaged properties** beyond the
  basic even-build rule; edge cases around mortgaging a property in a group
  with houses are disallowed (you must sell houses first), matching common
  house rules.
- **Income/Luxury Tax** use the standard fixed amounts ($200 / $100).

These simplifications are chosen because each removes an *interaction* loop
(auction, trade) rather than a *rule*, and none of them prevents a game from
being played to a winner.

