## 3. Module / File Breakdown

```
main.py                  # entry point: builds a game, runs the auto-play demo
monopoly/
    __init__.py          # package marker, version
    board.py             # space definitions + Board class (lookups, groups)
    cards.py             # Chance & Community Chest decks + card effects
    dice.py              # Dice abstraction (seeded RNG, doubles tracking)
    player.py            # Player state (cash, position, assets, jail status)
    game.py              # Game engine: turn loop, movement, rent, building,
                         #   jail, bankruptcy, win detection
    special_rules.py     # "Let's Get Rich" house rule (shared pot helpers)
    ai.py                # Decision policy for automated players
    render.py            # Text rendering of board/state/turn log
tests/
    test_game.py         # unit tests for engine invariants
README.md                # how to run, what to expect, rule subset summary
```

Rationale: `board`, `cards`, `dice`, `player` are pure data/state with no
cross-dependencies beyond `player` referencing board indices. `game` is the
only module that mutates world state and is the single place rules live.
`ai` and `render` depend on `game`/`player` but `game` never depends on them
(dependency inversion: the driver and view are pluggable). `main.py` wires
them together.

